      "body": "Thanks for looking into this, and for saying up front how you found it. I'm going to close it, because the current code already uses a fresh random IV for every encryption. Here's why:\r\n\r\n**The provider already generates the IV.** `cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key)` is called with no `AlgorithmParameterSpec`. The JCE contract says the provider must then generate the parameters itself, and SunJCE builds a 12-byte GCM IV from `SecureRandom`. We checked on JDK 21:\r\n\r\n```java\r\nCipher c = Cipher.getInstance(\"AES/GCM/NoPadding\");\r\nfor (int i = 0; i < 100_000; i++) { c.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key); ivs.add(hex(c.getIV())); c.doFinal(new byte[1]); }\r\n// distinct IVs: 100000/100000, all 12 bytes\r\n\r\nc.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key, new GCMParameterSpec(128, c.getIV()));\r\n// InvalidAlgorithmParameterException: Cannot reuse iv for GCM encryption\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo every call gets a new random IV, and the provider refuses to reuse one even when asked to. RVSec probably flagged this because the IV is created inside the provider, where an API-level runtime checker can't trace it back to a random source. It's a false positive, not a weakness.\r\n\r\n**The analysis and the change cover different code.** The patch changes `SecretEncryption.jvm.kt`, which is the **desktop** (`jvmMain`) actual. An Android run doesn't execute that file, and `:amethyst:assembleFdroidDebug` doesn't compile it, so neither the finding nor the build check reached the changed code. Android has its own actual, `SecretEncryption.android.kt`, backed by AndroidKeyStore. That actual has to let the provider choose the IV: keystore keys refuse an IV supplied by the caller unless `setRandomizedEncryptionRequired(false)` is set, which we don't want.\r\n\r\n**The patch also removes the per-thread `Cipher` cache** that mirrors the Android actual. A new `Cipher` and `SecureRandom` on every call is safe, but it adds cost and makes the two actuals diverge without improving security.\r\n\r\nPlease don't let this put you off. Crypto misuse in Android apps is a worthwhile thing to research, and if RVSec flags something else in Amethyst, I'm happy to look at it. For future reports, make sure to make a reproducing test against the actual run (here, something like a duplicate-IV assertion) helps a lot in telling a real finding from a tracing gap in the tool. Keeping tooling in hypotheticals, both when evaluating if bugs actually exist and checking if the proposed fix actually reaches the user interface, will only waste people's time. ",
